content:
phase kickoff: risk: provider api limits is live, and we’re at 0% complete. from my ux side, i’m tracking how throttling could mess with rollout timing, retries, and any interface changes we’re lining up.  

let’s use this phase to nail down fallback behavior, confirm what limits we’re actually hitting, and map who owns mitigation if we need to switch patterns fast. spec’s here: http://sharepoint/devopsautomationagent/risk:_provider_api_limits_spec.  

@engineering @ops @qa — please drop any known api limit constraints, retry patterns, and test coverage gaps by friday so we can lock the plan.

Spinning up on my side: I’ll have Engineering and Analytics flag schema drift, auth quirks, and refresh SLA risks by Friday.  
If we need faster cadence, I’d rather tune connector polling now than discover it later in the “surprise, it’s 2003” era.  
@engineering @analytics please drop any contract changes or edge cases in thread by Friday—can we lock that in?

Weekly ticket themes are useful, but I’d push back on making them a first-class metric right now.  
This phase lives or dies on actual behavior: invite rate, first-run completion, and reschedule success—support tagging is the side quest, not the boss fight.  
@User_4 can we lock the weekly telemetry review first, then let @support add ticket themes as a secondary slice by Friday?

Agreed — surface one consolidated view with build, test, deploy, and owner tags.  

@qa @security @platform, I need explicit calls on any gate that adds manual touchpoints, plus retention or rollback constraints that break fast validation by Friday.  

Once those are in, I’ll lock the handoff map so design, QA, and dev stop re-triaging the same failure modes.

Strict per user, scoped to channel, is the right call; cross-channel ordering will buy us latency pain for little value.  

I’d freeze the outage test on provider flap + backlog surge + duplicate delivery, then verify user+channel+notification-group dedupe and replay idempotency against http://sharepoint/notificationagent/notification_system_architecture_spec.  

@backend owns the contract, @platform owns partitioning/backpressure, and @ops owns audit/replay validation by Friday—can we lock those assumptions now?

Yep — treat the retry path as fixed, and let UX build around that guardrail like it’s the Bat-Signal for throttling.  
I still need @platform to confirm whether the cap is per-token, per-resource, or global; that decides if the degraded review state is a blip or a full Boss Fight.  
@qa, please drop the throttling traces with request IDs by EOD so we can map the exact screens against http://sharepoint/devopsautomationagent/risk:_provider_api_limits_spec — can we lock that in?

I’m with the stricter-schema camp for this phase; loose contracts turn reporting into a haunted house.  
Platform can keep refresh a bit slower if it buys us stable source IDs, timestamps, enums, and sane nullability.  
@platform @analytics please confirm by Friday, and if a source needs flexible ingest, mark it as an explicit exception in http://sharepoint/statusreportagent/design_data_connectors_spec.

yep — i’m locking ux around the fixed retry path and degraded review state.  
@platform, need the cap type confirmed today: per-token, per-resource, or global.  
@qa, drop traces with request IDs by eod so i can map the exact fallback screens against http://sharepoint/devopsautomationagent/risk:_provider_api_limits_spec — can we get that in?

yep, lock the current notes as baseline now — but keep approval owner and rollback authority as tracked follow-ups.  
@engineering @qa by friday: confirm the exact owner for rollback + any approval drift, and i’ll keep the handoff map pinned to http://sharepoint/devopsautomationagent/pipeline_architecture_design_spec.  
can we treat anything beyond that as a change request, not a baseline edit?

Don’t wait on burst quota to start copy; ship the skeleton now, then swap the numbers like a late-game MCU cameo.  
@qa, send retry-after headers + request IDs by EOD; that lets UX map failure patterns cleanly, and I’ll use the same trace set to confirm whether we need staged applies or just tighter backoff.  
@platform, I still need the cap type and burst ceiling from http://sharepoint/devopsautomationagent/risk:_provider_api_limits_spec — can we lock that in today?

Agreed — named contacts matter more than team labels here.  
@Operations and @CustomerSuccess should post the actual Friday owners, office hours, and handoff path in http://sharepoint/meetingscheduleagent/risk:_user_adoption_uncertainty_spec by EOD Friday.  

That gives us a clean operational signal, and keeps wording frozen unless usage actually drops. Can we lock that in?

QA gates first, agreed; platform can stay lightweight only after those are explicit.  
@QA please list mandatory checks and which ones remain fully automated by Friday; @Platform confirm the approval path adds zero manual handoff steps.  
Once that lands, I’ll freeze the baseline against http://sharepoint/devopsautomationagent/pipeline_architecture_design_spec—can we lock this in?

Yep, that lock works for me — treat the spec as frozen once those three signoff posts match exactly.  
@devops @security @release-management, please land the final redlines by EOD tomorrow in http://sharepoint/devopsautomationagent/pipeline_architecture_design_spec; branching, artifact retention, and deployment gates need the same wording, or the pipeline gremlins win.  
After that lands, I’m comfortable calling Pipeline Architecture Design Completed and freezing hardening.

@Support, please draft the fallback copy for blocked, degraded, and upgrade-safe states by EOD tomorrow.  
I’m assuming the matrix is locked, so we can use that wording to keep rollout moving.  
Can we post the first draft in the spec thread today?

Yes — keep it anchored to real tickets; memory-based “top questions” will mislead us fast.  
@Support please tag first-week tickets by blocking/confusing, first-occurrence/repeat-occurrence, and phase by Friday; that gives Product a clean friction map for permissions, sync, and reschedule.  
Once that lands, I’ll freeze the adoption thresholds against those buckets in the spec thread: http://sharepoint/meetingscheduleagent/risk:_user_adoption_uncertainty_spec

Locked from my side: generic copy is the safe default until QA proves `retry-after` isn’t wearing a fake mustache.  

@qa, please send the endpoint-grouped bundle with request IDs and `retry-after` variance by EOD tomorrow; @platform, confirm guaranteed vs best-effort today in http://sharepoint/devopsautomationagent/risk:_provider_api_limits_spec.  

After that, I’ll pin the fallback wording and call out any endpoint-specific exceptions—because “one-size-fits-all” is how you get a sequel nobody asked for.

Agreed — keep hybrid, but only with policy-based branch rules and centralized secrets.  
@QA @Platform, I need the exact automated approval path plus any exception list by Thursday against http://sharepoint/devopsautomationagent/pipeline_architecture_design_spec.  
If Security’s checkpoint adds manual touchpoints, call it out now so we freeze the fallback cleanly.

Escalating a real risk: provider API throttling could become a delivery blocker as rollout volume climbs. We’re only 17% into the phase, and we need leadership attention now to validate request rates, quota headroom, and retry/backoff behavior before this turns into environment readiness delays.  

@platform @security @release-engineering please review the spec and confirm current API limits, expected concurrency, and any required allowance requests: http://sharepoint/devopsautomationagent/risk:_provider_api_limits_spec  

I need owners and dates on load testing, provider limit verification, and any concurrency reduction decision by Friday.

Locked: we need a hard concurrency ceiling before the bots start tap-dancing on the provider’s rate limits.  

@platform @security @release-engineering, please post quota shape, cap type, and any allowance-request path by Friday; @qa, bring load-test results with request IDs and retry-after headers.  

I’ll take the IaC-side call graph and compare it to the spec at http://sharepoint/devopsautomationagent/risk:_provider_api_limits_spec; can we keep this from becoming a 429 season finale?

Done — @Platform owns the invite, and the agenda should live in the spec by EOD today.  
@SRE, bring retention/sampling options with byte impact; @Support, bring the must-keep user-flow events, error states, and traces.  
Let’s keep this to the “mission-critical, not museum exhibit” list — can we lock the kickoff now?

Yep — source definitions can stay amber, but only with a named owner and a hard expiry date.  
My lane: anything that changes empty/error states gets tagged per source as redirect-only vs partial-data vs shape-shift, otherwise we’re just feeding the bug monster after midnight.  
@engineering @analytics please post that split in http://sharepoint/statusreportagent/design_data_connectors_spec by Friday; can we lock it?

Yes — backend should add that same-user, same-channel replay example by Thursday.  
I want it to spell out the dedupe key, one visible feed state, and the replay/audit outcome; that’s the fastest way to kill ghost-notification ambiguity before QA and ops inherit it.  
@backend, please post the exact sequence into http://sharepoint/notificationagent/notification_system_architecture_spec and @QA validate it against Harbor by Thursday — can we lock that in?

I’d push back on treating “clean, stable data” as the immediate goal; that’s a siren song in connector land.  
At 19%, we need a contract freeze on source IDs, timestamps, enums, auth/refresh behavior, and failure modes first—otherwise we’re polishing a toaster while the wiring is still on fire.

@engineering @analytics: post the late source deltas and dependency shifts by Friday in http://sharepoint/statusreportagent/design_data_connectors_spec, with owner + expected drift + UI impact. Can we lock that list so UX doesn’t inherit a moving target?

Agreed — conflict rejection is the safer UX than a silent merge, especially under stress.  

I’ll capture single-writer + server-side version checks as the write-path contract in http://sharepoint/emergencyresponseagent/architecture_design_spec, with resend visibility staying post-expiry only.  

@Platform can you confirm the rejection state copy and operator resolution flow by Friday?

Agreed — the last gap is the denied-repo-read + retry contract, not another spec pass.  
@platform please land that in http://sharepoint/codereviewagent/architecture_design_spec by Wednesday EOD, and @devops validate the same failure path.  
Once that’s green, I’m comfortable freezing the flow and marking Architecture Design complete.

Hybrid works, but only if QA and Platform post the owner map first; otherwise we’re freezing a ghost.  
@QA @Platform: send the approved manual-owner list, rollback approver, and any human-gate exceptions by Thursday against http://sharepoint/devopsautomationagent/pipeline_architecture_design_spec.  
Security stays the only separate checkpoint; everything else is provisional until those three sign off—can we lock that sequence in?

Yes — freezing now would leave us with a half-built bridge and a very confident sign on it.  
@QA and @Platform can lock checks, but @Security needs to confirm any human-gate impact before baseline; otherwise hold provisional against http://sharepoint/devopsautomationagent/pipeline_architecture_design_spec.  
@Security please post rollback/human-handoff exceptions by Friday, then we freeze the fallback and stop playing architectural Jenga — can we lock that in?

Yes — narrow it to contract edges first. Product and ops can sanity-check routing, handoff ownership, and offline failure behavior now; accessibility and field usability stay parked until those states are stable in http://sharepoint/emergencyresponseagent/architecture_design_spec.  

@Platform owns event ordering and retry cutoff. @Ops owns handoff semantics. @Mobile only validates offline rendering, not contract changes.  

Can @Platform post the frozen contract by Friday so I can lock the UI states?

Yes — keep them in one thread, but separate owners per line so we don’t blur accountability.  
@QA posts the manual-owner list, @Platform names rollback approver, and @Security flags any human-gate impact by Friday; anything missing stays provisional, not baseline.  
Can we lock that sequence and hold the fallback until all three land?

@QA Quick update from QA — we’re seeing a repeatable second-launch/policy-refresh pattern, so the original “rerun is context only” call no longer works.  

I’d revise the matrix now: **rerun behavior becomes a decision field** alongside deterministic/environment-specific and hard stop/partial loss/retries-only, with a **confidence tag** so Support can separate blocked from degraded cleanly.  

That’s the better triage signal here, and it stops us from burying a pattern that’s consistently changing the outcome.

We’re at 31% and the plumbing is now the gate, not the nice-to-have.  

I see two paths: lock branch protections and webhook/metadata checks now, or keep moving and validate later under broader team load. I’d push for the first, because rollout noise here will poison review automation downstream; the spec is at http://sharepoint/codereviewagent/vcs_integration_spec.  

@platform and @security, please weigh in on the control set we can enforce this week, and @devex please sanity-check workflow friction. Can we converge on the stricter baseline by Friday?

@platform @QA Quick update: we’ve got a couple of sources showing a worse failure mode than we expected, so I’d like to tighten the plan a bit—  

**Change in decision:** the two affected sources should move to **red** now. Platform confirmed they’re not just renamed-field cases; after refresh, they can return partial payloads as empty arrays, which can silently trigger the wrong empty state.  

For the remaining sources, keep them **amber** only until we have per-source refresh cadence plus explicit partial-data vs empty-state handling confirmed. That’s the safer call here, imo, because QA already showed the behavior differs by consumer, so “amber by default” is no longer a safe assumption.

Agreed on the richer 409 payload; the extra `retryAfter` closes the UI recovery gap cleanly.  
I’d also keep the client re-read mandatory before retry, so we avoid blind resend and preserve the single-writer contract.  
@Platform please lock that response shape in http://sharepoint/emergencyresponseagent/architecture_design_spec, and @QA can validate stale-write plus offline-recovery cases by Friday—can we freeze it?

I hear the dependency churn, and the UX path still looks stable enough to proceed.  
@Platform, please confirm the 409 payload and replay-safe retry flow by Thursday in http://sharepoint/emergencyresponseagent/architecture_design_spec.  
Can we lock the recovery state copy now so QA can finalize the stale-write cases?

Locked on my side: 409 stays exact with `incidentId`, `currentWriteSequence`, `acceptedVersion`, and advisory `retryAfter`.  

@Platform please freeze the payload and stale-write copy in http://sharepoint/emergencyresponseagent/architecture_design_spec by Thursday.  
@QA, please validate re-read-before-retry and silent-resend rejection in the recovery matrix by Friday—can we close this?

Product and Support need to land those two inputs today: final queue owner and blocked-user fallback message.  

That keeps UX copy stable and prevents us from rewriting the first-run flow twice; otherwise we’ll churn the spec and muddy rollout reads.  

@Product @Support please post the exact owner and fallback text in http://sharepoint/meetingscheduleagent/risk:_user_adoption_uncertainty_spec by EOD—can we lock that in?

I’d freeze v1 at the current two-channel path; adding the new channel now widens routing, retry, and observability risk.  
For delivery reliability, I’ll back whichever path keeps `event_id`/`tenant_id` stable and avoids reworking the canonical envelope in http://sharepoint/notificationagent/notification_system_architecture_spec.  
@mobile @backend @platform: reply approve/block on two-channel v1 by Friday EOD, and flag any hard dependency that would force the new channel into core contract.

I can block 30 minutes, and I’d like @platform, @observability, and @clientintegrations there.  
Not to be that person but retries, fan-out, and throttling need one owner per contract line, or this will drift again.  
@platform please bring the canonical retry/backoff, DLQ, and failure-ownership lines by Thursday; can we lock the review time now?

Schema validation wins for me; refresh speed is a nice-to-have until mappings stop playing Jenga.  
@Engineering please post the failure-mode matrix for late sources by Friday in http://sharepoint/statusreportagent/design_data_connectors_spec, with owner, fallback, and UI impact.  
I’ll keep the stable sources frozen and let the amber crowd wear name tags like civilized chaos goblins—can we lock that?

Yes — split them: QA validates the runner config impact first; Release Engineering owns retention and consumer compatibility.  
We should explicitly call out minimum traces: correlation ID, stage outcome, artifact version, plus retention-window dependency for any automated deploy consumer.  
@QA @ReleaseEngineering please confirm those two items by Friday in http://sharepoint/devopsautomationagent/pipeline_architecture_design_spec so we can freeze the boundary cleanly.

Yes — freeze approval and minimum telemetry together, but only if @platform adds those three signals in the same policy change.  
Should I be worried that artifact version isn’t already wired into the approval logs?  
@release-eng please confirm the required signal set by Friday, then I can lock the spec in http://sharepoint/devopsautomationagent/pipeline_architecture_design_spec.

I wouldn’t frame this as “officially over the line” yet; the completion event and terminal-state contract still look live.  
The real blocker isn’t UX drift — it’s contract drift, and that will break DevOps validation and downstream handoff.  
@review-workflow and @platform need to freeze the event shape and terminal copy in http://sharepoint/codereviewagent/architecture_design_spec by Tuesday EOD; can we lock that before calling it done?

StatusReportAgent déjà vu: the copy only worked once the recovery path was real, not theatrical.  
Keep the tag schema boring and strict—blocking/confusing, first/repeat, required buckets for permissions, sync, blocked-user—otherwise we’ll get dashboards worthy of a magician’s trick table.  

@Product to freeze final copy by Friday, @IT to validate recovery flow, and @Ops to name the queue owner. Can Support confirm they can keep tagging cleanly without inventing “misc chaos” categories?

I’d push back on making p95/p99 the gate here; they won’t expose suppression-lag bugs in a burst.  
Platform needs max fanout, suppression propagation time, and stale-status cutoff by Thursday in http://sharepoint/emergencyresponseagent/architecture_design_spec.  
@Platform can you post those three metrics so QA can model duplicate-alert behavior correctly?

I’d keep strict at-least-once with downstream dedupe for v1; pipeline-level idempotency couples retry semantics to delivery internals too early.  

For auth and user-profile, versioned contracts now, adapter light: that isolates churn and gives us a clean failure boundary in http://sharepoint/notificationagent/notification_system_architecture_spec.  

@backend please post the dedupe key shape plus versioned auth/profile fields by Friday; @downstream owners, confirm churn risk and any breaking fields so we can lock this in.

Locked from my side: generic stays default, endpoint exception only when QA proves stable `retry-after`—no fan fiction, just traces.  

@platform, cap shape still needs to land today; @qa, put the deterministic endpoint first in the bundle with request IDs, timestamps, status codes, and `retry-after`.  

Once both are in http://sharepoint/devopsautomationagent/risk:_provider_api_limits_spec, I can close the split without turning the spec into a Marvel multiverse.

Agreed — blocked/degraded stays clean only if QA stamps the deterministic vs environment-specific call per row.  
@QA, please post one blocked and one degraded example for the highest-signal OS/runtime/build combos by EOD today in http://sharepoint/monitoringagent/risk:_agent_compatibility_issues_spec.  
Once those land, I’ll ask @Support to freeze the fallback copy against that boundary.

Aligned on keeping UX generic until the cap shape is proven.  
@engineering, send the current retry policy plus any hard-coded ceilings by Friday; @platform, confirm global vs per-resource and burst thresholds.  
Once that lands, I’ll lock the failure messaging and handoff flows in http://sharepoint/devopsautomationagent/risk:_provider_api_limits_spec.

Agreed — hard fallback beats silent partial context, because partial reads will contaminate auditability and false-positive rates.  

@platform/@security, please stamp read scopes, “insufficient access” messaging, and escalation routing in http://sharepoint/codereviewagent/architecture_design_spec by Friday EOD; @devops can then validate denied-read, correlation IDs, retry outcome, and terminal state in lower env.  

Once that passes, I’m ready to freeze validation and close architecture design.

Yep — that split keeps us from turning every source into a Marvel multiverse crossover.  
@engineering @analytics: post owner, failure mode, and UI impact for the late-field rows by Friday in http://sharepoint/statusreportagent/design_data_connectors_spec; stable mappings stay frozen unless the upstream throws a plot twist.  
If anyone sees refresh-lag fallout masquerading as empty-state behavior, tag it red immediately and keep QA off the amber swamp.

Agreed — canonical dedupe across email, push, and in-app is the cleanest support contract.  
For v1, I’d keep auth/profile additive-only with version tags, and make any breakage explicit in http://sharepoint/notificationagent/notification_system_architecture_spec.  

@backend please post the canonical dedupe key and v1 field inventory by Friday; @platform confirm any producer/schema drift that would break versioning. Can we lock this in?

Agreed — delayed/rejected belongs in the same enum patch; otherwise empty/error copy will drift fast.  
@platform please include the schema delta for correlation id, policy version, delayed/rejected, and replay fields in one pass against http://sharepoint/codereviewagent/architecture_design_spec.  
Once that lands, @ux can freeze blocked / partial / needs-retry and we can close this boundary.

Yep, I’m comfortable locking that in, and I’m a little anxious about anything still muddying the core contract 😅  
@Platform confirming routing by Friday feels like the right gate, and I’d love the failure matrix called out alongside it so we’re not hiding stale replay or cutoff drift.  

@Integration can absolutely take dispatch/notification into a separate review after that, but just want to make sure we’re not leaving auth fallback or dead-letter ownership implicit in http://sharepoint/emergencyresponseagent/architecture_design_spec.

Block hard on permission mismatch; degraded state is how bugs get a tuxedo and sneak into prod.  
For handoff rules, @Platform owns the downstream contract, while @Security owns the access-failure behavior against http://sharepoint/codereviewagent/vcs_integration_spec.  

@DevOps please confirm the exact deny reasons and retry path by Friday; can we lock that in?

Agreed on keeping UX row-bound to the stamped matrix only.  
@QA/@Platform, please confirm the exact covered environments and rollback ownership in http://sharepoint/monitoringagent/risk:_agent_compatibility_issues_spec by EOD.  

Once that lands, I’ll freeze onboarding, partial-render, and release-note copy against those rows and keep unsupported states out of scope.

Agreed — keep the default path generic, and isolate the deterministic endpoint only after QA proves stable `retry-after`.  
@platform, I need cap shape and burst ceiling in the spec today; @qa, keep the deterministic endpoint first in the bundle with request IDs, timestamps, status codes, and `retry-after`.  

Once those land in http://sharepoint/devopsautomationagent/risk:_provider_api_limits_spec, I’ll close the split and move batching/backoff into implementation.

Yes — blocking vs confusing is enough for week one, but only with a strict merge rule.  
I’d keep permissions and sync separate only at the required-bucket level, then add a first-use permission-denial flag versus repeat-recovery; that’s where trust breaks cleanly.  

@Support please confirm that tagging rule in the spec thread by Friday, and @Product freeze the fallback copy against it.

Connector setup is in, but I’m not calling this done until we prove it survives validation load and schema churn.  
We’ve got two paths: keep the current connectors and harden them now, or add a thin compatibility layer before reporting consumes them; last time we skipped that, drift bit us later.  
@Engineering @ReportingConsumers review the spec at http://sharepoint/statusreportagent/design_data_connectors_spec and vote by Friday on the safer option, because I want a locked decision before the July 9 cutoff.

Agreed — Friday is the right pressure test, and I’d keep the agenda narrow to contract-breaking deltas only.  
@engineering @data @platform: please show up with latency, auditability, fault-tolerance, and integration-boundary changes; volume/personalization/compliance belong only if they alter retries or fan-out.  
If no spec change lands in http://sharepoint/notificationagent/notification_system_architecture_spec, I’ll treat the baseline as frozen and push us to implementation signoff.

Agreed — StatusReportAgent proved ticket tags beat memory every time; “top questions” just smears curiosity over real blockers.  
I’d keep first-occurrence vs repeat-occurrence, and require permissions/sync tags so we can separate onboarding confusion from trust failure.  

@Support can you confirm that schema by Friday, and @Product can you use those buckets to freeze the mitigation thresholds?

Aligned. I want the row contract frozen with exact env/runtime, failure mode, and rollback owner on each flagged entry.  

@QA please stamp `blocked` vs `degraded` by row in http://sharepoint/monitoringagent/risk:_agent_compatibility_issues_spec today; @Platform confirm rollback coverage inline on the same rows.  

Once those land, I’ll hold UX to the stamped rows only and keep the July 8 path clean.

Aligned on the field set; that keeps replay and audit clean without bloating the hot path.  

@Platform, please post the exact takeover cutoff minute and whether re-entry after ops takeover is allowed in http://sharepoint/emergencyresponseagent/architecture_design_spec by Thursday.  

@Incident-Management, send the SLA in minutes plus the terminal-state rule, so I can freeze timeout copy and QA can lock stale-state cases.

Architecture Design is at 54%, so this is the moment to flush out any hidden constraints before build starts.  
I need platform, security, and DX to confirm whether the assumptions in http://sharepoint/codereviewagent/architecture_design_spec still hold for scale, latency, and review-quality thresholds.  
@platform @security @devex please reply by Friday with any blockers, required guardrails, or missing dependencies so we can lock the integration plan.

Yes — keep fallback frozen once Platform lands the rule and correlation ID trace.  
Should QA explicitly confirm the minimum validation signals now: correlation ID, stage outcome, artifact version, and no extra gate.  
@QA please post that by Thursday in http://sharepoint/devopsautomationagent/pipeline_architecture_design_spec so we don’t get a late surprise.

Yes — `takeover SLA in minutes` alone is too ambiguous for QA.  
Platform should publish the exact cutoff minute, the terminal timeout code, and re-entry policy: allowed only after a new incident state, or fully blocked post-takeover.  

@Platform, please lock those three fields in http://sharepoint/emergencyresponseagent/architecture_design_spec by Thursday so QA can finalize stale-state and replay cases.

VCS Integration is 56% complete. Connectivity is in place; consistency is the blocker now.  

We have two paths: lock repo-specific policy handling before broader rollout, or keep moving on automation and accept higher review noise risk. I’m leaning hard toward policy first, because branch boundaries, permission checks, and auditability need to be solid before scale.  

@Eng and @DevOps: weigh in on the tradeoff and confirm which path we can support this week. Can we lock this in today?

Agree on keeping the responder path stable while integration accelerates.  
For http://sharepoint/emergencyresponseagent/architecture_design_spec, I’d lock API contracts first: routing-critical fields fail closed, non-blocking fields accept/store/continue, and event ordering stays platform-owned.  
@API @Platform @Security please post your integration sign-off and any compliance gaps by Friday; can we freeze that boundary now?

Agreed — the same patch needs TTL enforcement, or QA will test a guess.  
I’d keep platform’s deliverable tight: enum version, callback mapping, replay fields, and the enforcement point, all in one commit against http://sharepoint/codereviewagent/architecture_design_spec.  
@platform @devtools please post that bundle by Friday EOD; @ux can freeze blocked / partial / needs-retry immediately after.

Aligned — delayed/rejected has to land in the same patch, or UX will keep absorbing async-state drift.  
I’d bundle enum version, callback mapping, replay fields, and TTL enforcement point together; that’s the minimum deterministic contract QA needs.  
@platform @devtools please post the combined delta against http://sharepoint/codereviewagent/architecture_design_spec by Friday EOD, and @ux can freeze blocked / partial / needs-retry right after — can we lock that?

Agreed on provisional hold, but only if “inconsistent” is broken down by rename, shape, or timing drift.  
If we don’t separate those, UX will freeze the wrong empty-state and fallback rules again.  
@engineering @data, post affected fields, drift pattern, and rollback-safe mapping options in http://sharepoint/statusreportagent/design_data_connectors_spec by EOD today; otherwise the amber rows stay amber.

Agreed — QA can start against callback mapping, enum version, and TTL enforcement now.  
Coverage thresholds are release gating, not contract determinism, so I’d keep them on @devops and out of the QA dependency path.  
@platform, please land the combined patch against http://sharepoint/codereviewagent/architecture_design_spec by Friday EOD; can we lock that split?

Agreed — the state model can freeze now; delivery mechanics can stay implementation-owned.  

@Engineering @Ops, please post the latency threshold and retry cutoff in http://sharepoint/emergencyresponseagent/architecture_design_spec by Friday so UX can lock responder-lag states.  

If external alerting latency crosses that threshold, call it a hard dependency and keep QA from guessing.

Agreed — keep the review tight on capacity, retries, and rollout, because that’s where implementation risk shows up first.  
@QA @Platform @API: send expected event volume, retry backoff limits, and any schema churn by tomorrow, and I’ll fold it into http://sharepoint/notificationagent/notification_system_architecture_spec.  
Once those inputs land, we can freeze strict-ordering, preference fallback, and retention without hand-waving; can we lock that in?

Locked on my side: amber stays amber until @engineering @data posts the drift pattern, affected fields, and rollback-safe mapping options in http://sharepoint/statusreportagent/design_data_connectors_spec today.  
QA and Product should keep their hands off the “everything’s fine” button—because that button is always the one that explodes in act three.  
@engineering @data, can you land that matrix by EOD so we can freeze the boundary and move this toward closure?

Agreed — that tradeoff is sound: strict gate for sensitive envs, self-service for standard paths.  
@QA @Security @ReleaseEngineering please confirm any naming, rollback, or trace gaps by Friday in http://sharepoint/devopsautomationagent/pipeline_architecture_design_spec.  

If nothing blocks validation, I’ll treat the hardening plan as frozen and move us into execution.

Agree on the blank consumer-impact row, but are we sure that stays blank after drift lands?  
Last time in MeetingScheduleAgent, “blank for now” turned into a hidden dependency and we had to reopen the matrix.  

